LETTERBOX on

TITLE night_shift_title

♪ A single held organ note, recorded in a stairwell, badly. ♪

SHOT
An unofficial sequel to a picture the studio did not own, shot in eleven nights in a foundry that was still working days. Everything you see is real and nothing you hear is.
SHOT_END

SLUG NUMBER FOUR FOUNDRY | THIRD SHIFT

SHOT
A woman signs a book at a gate under a lamp with moths in it. This is ADA DELPH and she has never held a ladle in her life.
SHOT_END

NARRATOR:
They were forty hands short that winter and they were not fussy about it.

ACT 1: The New Hand

SHOT
The floor. Heat haze rendered by shooting through a fire, which is the sort of thing Prine did instead of paying for an effect.
SHOT_END

FOREMAN:
You stand there. You don't stand there. Anything comes off the ladle, you don't look at it, you move.

DELPH:
What was here before me?

FOREMAN:
A man.

DELPH:
Where'd he go?

SHOT
The Foreman does not answer. He signs something and walks out of frame, and the camera stays on the doorway he left through for eleven full seconds.
SHOT_END

♪ The organ note has not stopped. It has not stopped since the titles. ♪

ACT 2: The Count

SLUG THE POUR | 0340

SHOT
The ladle tips. White metal into a mould, and the whole top of the frame goes to bare light.
SHOT_END

NARRATOR:
There is a thing every foundry hand knows and no foundry will write down: the pour makes a sound, and the sound is different on a night when the count is wrong.

SHOT
DELPH, at the rail, listening. She has been on the floor four nights and she has already started to listen.
SHOT_END

DELPH:
That's a different note.

FOREMAN:
That's the same note it's been for thirty years.

DELPH:
It's a different note.

SHOT
Close on the mould. The metal, settling. And in the settling — for two frames, and only two, and they are two of the most argued-about frames in Coldwater cinema — something that is not a defect in the casting.
SHOT_END

TECH_DIFFICULTIES 7

SHOT
The tape you are watching has been copied at least four times and the two frames are gone. They are gone from almost every copy. Ask the man who rented it to you; he will tell you, at length, which prints still have them.
SHOT_END

INTERMISSION 40

ACT 3: The Reading

SLUG THE OFFICE | BEFORE DAWN

SHOT
A ledger open on a desk. Names in a column. A great many names.
SHOT_END

DELPH:
Sixty-one names and none of them clocked out.

FOREMAN:
They were reassigned.

DELPH:
To WHERE.

FOREMAN:
There's a form. It goes up. It doesn't come back.

SHOT
She looks at him for a long time. He does not look away, and Prine holds it until it stops being a stand-off and becomes something sadder.
SHOT_END

FOREMAN:
I've asked.

SLUG THE FLOOR | THE LAST POUR OF THE SHIFT

SHOT
DELPH at the rail. The ladle coming over. The light coming up.
SHOT_END

NARRATOR:
She stayed eleven years.

NARRATOR:
She never once got used to the note.

FADE out 3

SHOT
The gate. The book. The lamp. The moths. A new hand signing in, whose face we do not see.
SHOT_END

FADE in 2

